# Energy flow and other chart families

Use each chart for its real question. A distribution diagram shows how energy moves between sources and uses. A gauge shows one value against a scale. A pie shows parts of a defined whole. Styling must not turn one of these into another, and must not suggest support the companion does not give.

These examples are simulated with invented values. They are not a screenshot of a home and not a new distribution engine. This page covers the design contract (HA-CTRL-012, from gap HA-GAP-012).

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-012-capabilities">Task and capability contract</h2>

| Chart family | Scope to state |
| --- | --- |
| Native Energy distribution | Keep its native name, direction, selected period and units. It is not an ONS Sankey implementation. |
| Native Sankey cards | These are separate native cards where available. Their presence in the source does not add them to the list of hosts the companion adapts. |
| Gauge | Keep the numeric value, unit, bounds and any threshold meaning that was set explicitly. |
| Pie or ring | Say what the whole is and which period it covers. Keep missing categories. Do not invent an “Other” category or drop small ones to fit the palette. |
| Third-party renderer | Record the card or engine version, its real controls, its data route and its theme support. |
| Supported Energy line or bar card | Follow the exact adapter contract, the shared identity colours and the 1.5px line weight. Do not extend that coverage to other charts. |

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-012-states">States, pending work and failure</h2>

A missing flow is not a zero flow. A direction arrow does not show a physical connection right now. Keep energy over the selected period (kWh) apart from current power (kW). A valve known to be closed does not show a measured zero flow. Keep partial coverage visible. Do not invent a residual to make inputs and outputs balance.

For a gauge outside its nominal range, keep the actual value and explain the scale. Do not clip the text silently. Take severity bands from an explicit definition, never from the brand palette. If a pie’s whole is incomplete, say so in the heading or caption. An accessible label cannot repair a misleading 100%.

Where the card structure or a series is unsupported, keep the native rendering. If a diagram fails or a table is missing, report that as a compatibility finding. Do not claim that theme variables make it conform.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-012-content">Content and interpretation</h2>

Name nodes and direction in words: “Grid to home”, not “Blue to purple”. Keep the native source labels, units, period and attribution. Say whether a figure is instantaneous or accumulated over the period. The reference flow table is a text review of invented values.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-012-accessibility">Accessibility and interaction</h2>

Check each node’s text value, name and direction without relying on moving particles. Respect reduced motion. If the native diagram cannot stop non-essential motion, record that limit for the version you tested. Provide, or point to, an equivalent route to the same values. One current-value summary is not an equivalent for a whole period’s graph. Test the diagram’s native focus order and overlapping labels at 320px.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-012-integration">Native integration boundary</h2>

The companion adapts only the native Energy Cartesian line and bar hosts listed in the [Compatibility register and native fallback](compatibility-register.html). Distribution, gauges, pies, Sankey cards and third-party engines stay outside it. Do not add broad selectors or promise the Energy View data table for them. Any extension needs its own data model, renderer API, stable colour and legend plan, fallback and clean-up evidence. [Charts and tables](../components/charts.html) owns the maintained boundary.

